<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ilya Repin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1844">1844</a>–<a href="/years/1930">1930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ilya Yefimovich Repin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ilya Repin was a Ukrainian-born Russian painter, born in Chuguev, who became one of the best-known artists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the 19th century. After study at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-academy">Imperial Academy</a> of Arts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, a commission from Grand Duke Vladimir Alexandrovich for Barge Haulers on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> (1873) launched his career. His works include Religious Procession in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a> Province, Ivan the Terrible and His Son Ivan (1885), which caused a scandal, and Reply of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaporozhian-cossacks">Zaporozhian Cossacks</a>, and portraits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modest-mussorgsky">Modest Mussorgsky</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a>. In 1898 he built a country house, the Penates, at Kuokkala in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, where he died in 1930.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ilya Repin</b>'s painting Barge Haulers on the Volga shows such burlaks at their labour in Tsarist Russia.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-song-of-the-volga-boatmen">The Song of the Volga Boatmen</a></li></ul></section>
